{"type":"cards","design":"S66_ISSUE_ROWS","title":"Cloud Run scaling and cold-start mitigation","lead":"Configured minimum instances and CPU allocation keep container dispatch under 10 ms.","items":[{"title":"Cold start latency spikes","fix":"Configure minimum warm instances across both Google Cloud regions; Enable startup CPU boost for faster container runtime initialization; Optimize container images using distroless bases to shrink pulls. These steps eliminate cold starts and keep response latencies consistently low.","status":"warning","icon":"analytics"},{"title":"AlloyDB connection pool exhaustion","fix":"Enforce strict maximum instance limits per Cloud Run service; Deploy AlloyDB Auth Proxy with managed connection pooling enabled; Tune application database pool sizes to match instance concurrency. This architecture prevents backend connection spikes and guards cluster stability.","status":"warning","icon":"security"},{"title":"Regional failover capacity shortfall","fix":"Provision symmetrical minimum instance counts in both active regions; Execute scheduled regional failover tests under simulated peak load; Configure Cloud Load Balancing with proactive capacity health checks. This ensures warm capacity instantly absorbs re-routed user workloads.","status":"warning","icon":"maximize"}],"notes":"Cloud Run provides a standard single-region Monthly Uptime Percentage SLA of 99.95% for non-GPU services, and deploying across two active regions behind Global External Application Load Balancing enables multi-region composite availability. Configured min-instances keep warm execution environments ready, mitigating cold starts down to sub-10ms container dispatch.

